1
00:00:00,900 --> 00:00:05,775
The brush uncovers a square stone tablet, carved with symbols around a central panel.

2
00:00:05,775 --> 00:00:12,820
It is an ayagapata, a votive tablet, and tablets like it were set up at Mathura by Jain donors two thousand years ago.

3
00:00:17,700 --> 00:00:24,571
As with the last episode, the revered figure is not drawn here — the tablet is shown for its symbols and its inscription.

4
00:00:27,000 --> 00:00:31,358
The Jain canon, which was compiled and written down over a long period.

5
00:00:31,358 --> 00:00:37,047
Inscriptions, including donor records that are among the most useful we have for this period.

6
00:00:37,047 --> 00:00:39,163
And excavation at Jain sites.

7
00:00:40,933 --> 00:00:46,951
Jain tradition counts twenty-four Tirthankaras — the word means ford-makers, those who make a crossing.

8
00:00:46,951 --> 00:00:49,844
The first is Rishabhanatha, also called Adinatha.

9
00:00:49,844 --> 00:00:51,811
The twenty-third is Parshvanatha,

10
00:00:51,811 --> 00:00:58,764
placed by tradition in the ninth century BCE and regarded by many historians as likely to have been a real figure.

11
00:01:04,267 --> 00:01:11,757
The twenty-fourth is Mahavira — and the important point is the one students most often get wrong: Mahavira did not found Jainism.

12
00:01:11,757 --> 00:01:15,802
He reformed and systematised a tradition that was already there.

13
00:01:20,367 --> 00:01:24,712
Mahavira was born Vardhamana, near Vaishali, into a ganasangha family.

14
00:01:24,712 --> 00:01:32,661
He left home at about thirty, practised extreme austerity for roughly twelve years, and attained kevala, complete knowledge.

15
00:01:37,233 --> 00:01:43,601
He was then called Mahavira, the great hero, and Jina, the conqueror — from which his followers take their name.

16
00:01:43,601 --> 00:01:47,001
He taught for about thirty years and died at Pavapuri.

17
00:01:51,300 --> 00:01:55,221
The teaching is tightly organised, which makes it examinable.

18
00:01:55,221 --> 00:01:59,252
Three jewels: right faith, right knowledge, right conduct.

19
00:02:03,267 --> 00:02:10,064
And five vows: ahimsa, non-violence; satya, truth; asteya, not taking what is not given; aparigraha,

20
00:02:10,064 --> 00:02:17,130
non-possession; and brahmacharya, chastity, which Mahavira added to the four attributed to Parshvanatha.

21
00:02:17,130 --> 00:02:24,159
Jain ahimsa is the strictest in Indian tradition, and it shapes everything from diet to occupation.

22
00:02:25,933 --> 00:02:32,138
And one idea worth more than a line, because it is Jainism's distinctive contribution to philosophy:

23
00:02:32,138 --> 00:02:34,963
anekantavada, the doctrine of many-sidedness.

24
00:02:34,963 --> 00:02:39,993
Reality has many aspects, and any single viewpoint captures only part of it.

25
00:02:41,767 --> 00:02:46,908
The companion idea, syadvada, holds that every statement is true only in some respect.

26
00:02:46,908 --> 00:02:51,700
It is an argument for intellectual humility built into the religion itself.

27
00:02:59,200 --> 00:03:05,822
The two main sects are the Digambaras, sky-clad, whose monks renounce clothing, and the Svetambaras, white-clad.

28
00:03:05,822 --> 00:03:10,810
Tradition explains the split through a famine in Magadha and a migration south.

29
00:03:15,967 --> 00:03:17,939
Some historians accept that outline;

30
00:03:17,939 --> 00:03:21,938
others argue the separation developed gradually over several centuries and

31
00:03:21,938 --> 00:03:25,883
that the famine story is a later explanation imposed on a slower process.

32
00:03:25,883 --> 00:03:32,101
The sects also differ on whether a woman can attain liberation in that life, and on which texts are canonical.

33
00:03:36,367 --> 00:03:39,605
Here is the memory tip, carved in stone.

34
00:03:39,605 --> 00:03:40,710
Three jewels.

35
00:03:40,710 --> 00:03:41,579
Five vows.

36
00:03:41,579 --> 00:03:43,853
Twenty-four ford-makers.

37
00:03:47,800 --> 00:03:48,532
Dig Quiz.

38
00:03:48,532 --> 00:03:49,922
Pause and name it.

39
00:03:49,922 --> 00:03:57,834
First: the twenty-third of the twenty-four, placed by tradition in the ninth century BCE. Parshvanatha.

40
00:04:00,600 --> 00:04:05,019
Second: the vow Mahavira added to the four already in the tradition.

41
00:04:05,019 --> 00:04:05,916
Brahmacharya.

42
00:04:05,916 --> 00:04:11,725
Third: the doctrine that reality has many aspects and no single view captures it all.

43
00:04:14,833 --> 00:04:15,817
Anekantavada.

44
00:04:15,817 --> 00:04:20,894
In Mains, a good question asks you to compare Buddhism and Jainism.

45
00:04:23,433 --> 00:04:29,294
Both reject Vedic sacrifice and the authority of birth; Jainism is far stricter on non-violence and austerity,

46
00:04:29,294 --> 00:04:33,132
and its doctrine of many-sidedness has no close Buddhist parallel.

47
00:04:37,300 --> 00:04:43,714
A votive tablet takes its place in the gallery: a gift recorded in stone by someone who wanted to be remembered giving it.

48
00:04:47,133 --> 00:04:52,662
Next gallery: the other thinkers of this age, and the armies arriving from the north-west.

49
00:04:52,662 --> 00:04:56,425
To catch it when it's out, subscribe to Gyaan-X Studios.

